**The short version, for standing at the printer.** Check only the surfaces that touch something. That means bearing seats, shaft bores, screw holes, belt paths and faces that bolt flat to another part. Looks don't matter.

**Reprint a part if a load-carrying wall is cracked or split between layers. Reprint it if a bearing seat or mating face can't be made to fit with the docs' fixes. Otherwise, use it.**

## What the official docs say

- **Tolerances:** bearing seats and shafts are designed for a well-calibrated printer. A seat that is too tight is reamed; one that is too loose is shimmed with tape or a drop of epoxy. Test the fit of the tapered bearings in their seats before assembling a joint ([Printing the parts](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/printing/)).
- **Screws:** four M3 brass inserts are melted in; everything else screws directly into the plastic ([Printing the parts](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/printing/)). So every screw hole has to bite.
- **Bearings seated square:** a crooked tapered bearing makes the joint stiff one way and loose the other ([Mechanical assembly](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/mechanical-assembly/)).
- **Pulleys:** check the first printed pulley against a belt before printing the rest ([Printing the parts](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/printing/)).

**The docs give no numbers:** no hole tolerance, no flatness limit, no rule on cosmetic defects. Everything below marked **ours** is our own judgement.

## How to check, with what

- **Bearing seat:** the real test is the docs' test-fit with the bearing itself. It should press in square by hand or with light, even pressure, and not rock. Calipers across the seat in two directions show an oval: **ours**, a difference over about 0.2 mm is worth a closer look.
- **Screw holes:** run the actual screw in. It should cut its thread and hold. A hole a blob has closed can be cleared with a drill of the hole's size. A hole that spins the screw freely needs a longer screw, epoxy, or a reprint (**ours**).
- **Mating faces:** lay the face on a flat table. **Ours:** if it rocks, or a sheet of paper slides under a corner, look for warp. A slight rock from a brim or support scar is fixed with a deburring tool or a file.
- **Belt paths and covers:** the belt and the wires have to pass without rubbing. Test-fit the cover over the belt before closing it up (**ours**).
- **Layers:** flex the part gently by hand along its tallest walls. A good part doesn't creak or open at a layer line (**ours**).

## Cosmetic and fine

**All ours.** None of these change how a part works:

- colour changes between spools, or a different colour on one part;
- support scars on faces that don't touch anything (clean them with a deburring tool if they catch);
- light stringing (pick it off or flash it with a heat gun);
- visible layer lines, and the seam line on curved walls;
- a slightly rough underside where tree supports touched.

## Part by part

### J1 turret (plate 7)

**What it holds** (building instructions): J2's motor and gearbox (p.60) and J2's two NSK HR32907J tapered bearings (p.70). It also carries the J1 motor (p.66) and the J1 stopper rails (p.72). It bolts onto `J1_rotation_shaft` with 6 × M4 16 (p.65).

**Check:**

- the J2 bearing seats, with a bearing test-fit;
- the six M4 holes into the rotation shaft;
- the motor and gearbox mounting holes;
- the face that sits on the rotation shaft, for flatness.

**The pause line at layer 247.** Plate 7b paused for about a minute at layer 247 when the filament ran out, then carried on with the same PETG HF.
- **Where it is:** 49.4 mm above the bed face, about two-thirds of the way up. At that height the layer crosses the tall block's two side walls and the small post. It doesn't cut through a bore.
- **Does it carry load:** yes (**ours**). The tall block is the structure that carries J2, so treat that layer as structural.
- **How to check:** look and feel around the part at 49.4 mm for a line, a step or a gap. Run a fingernail across it. Squeeze the two side walls gently toward each other and listen for a creak.
- **Verdict (ours):** a faint line with no gap is cosmetic. The nozzle and bed stayed hot through the pause. A gap you can see light through, or one that opens when you squeeze, means reprint.

### Plate 8: the small shoulder parts

- **J1 turret motor holder:** holds J2's 60 mm stepper and 20:1 gearbox (p.60). The gearbox's centring face and screw holes matter most. The shaft coupler has to line up, so the motor must sit square.
- **J1 backplate:** 6 × M4 16 (p.82), and all the wires except the J1 sensor's pass through it. Check the six holes and that the wire opening is clear of stringing and supports.
- **J1 belt cover and J1 wires cover:** M2 × 10 screws (p.77). The J1 wires run through the belt cover. Check the screw holes and that the belt runs free under the cover. The rest is cosmetic.
- **J2 limit-switch cover:** the switch has to be pressed by the moving part before the joint reaches its stop ([Mechanical assembly](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/mechanical-assembly/)). Check that the switch sits where the drawing puts it and its screw holes hold.
- **J2 stopper block** (moved to the next plate; p.68): the stop face takes the knock if the joint overruns. Check that face for cracks and the screw holes for grip.

### Every other part

Use the same three questions:

1. **What does it touch?** Find its page in the building instructions.
2. **Does that surface fit?** Test-fit the bearing, screw, belt or neighbouring part.
3. **Can the docs' fixes make it fit?** Ream tight seats, shim loose ones.

**Pulleys only:** test the first one against a belt, per the docs.

## Terms

- **bearing seat**: the printed bore a bearing presses into. The docs say to test-fit it, ream it if tight and shim it if loose ([Printing the parts](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/printing/)).
- **mating face**: a flat face that bolts against another part or a motor. If it isn't flat, the part it holds sits crooked (**ours**).
